oms in communities along a salinity gradient. Environ. Microbiol. 2<b>:</b>217-226.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11220307</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>N&#xfc;bel, U., F. Garcia-Pichel, and G. Muyzer.</b> 2000. The halotolerance and phylogeny of cyanobacteria with tightly coiled trichomes (<i>Spirulina turpin</i>) and the description of <i>Halospirulina tapeticola</i> gen. nov., sp. nov. Int. J. Syst. Evol. Microbiol. 50<b>:</b>1265-1277.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10843072</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Ollivier, B., P. Caumette, J. L. Garcia, and R. A. Mah.</b> 1994. Anaerobic bacteria from hypersaline environments. Microbiol. Rev. 58<b>:</b>27-38.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC372951</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8177169</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Oremland, R. S., and D. G. Capone.</b> 1988. Use of &#x201c;specific&#x201d; inhibitors in biogeochemistry and microbial ecology. Adv. Microb. Ecol. 10<b>:</b>285-383.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Oren, A.</b> 1999. Bioenergetic aspects of halophilism. Microbiol. Mol. Biol. Rev. 63<b>:</b>334-348.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC98969</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10357854</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Oren, A.</b> 2002. Diversity of halophilic microorganisms: environments, phylogeny, physiology, and applications. J. Ind. Microbiol. Biotechnol. 28<b>:</b>55-63.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11938472</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Oren, A., M. K&#xfc;hl, and U. Karsten.</b> 1995. An endoevaporitic microbial mat within a gypsum crust: zonation of phototrophs, photopigments, and light penetration. Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser. 128<b>:</b>151-159.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation><b>Revsbech, N. P., B. B. J&#xf8;rgen